[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Load Balancing Algorithms: Round Robin, Least Connections, and IP Hash"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In distributed systems and modern web architecture, load balancers act as the traffic cops sitting in front of your servers. They route client requests across all servers capable of fulfilling those requests, ensuring no single server bears too much demand. Choosing the right load balancing algorithm is critical for optimizing resource utilization, maximizing throughput, reducing latency, and ensuring fault tolerance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Round Robin"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Round Robin algorithm is one of the simplest and most common methods for distributing client requests. It works by cycling through a list of servers in a sequential order. When a request comes in, the load balancer assigns it to the first server on the list, then hands the next request to the second server, and so on, wrapping back around to the top of the list once the end is reached."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Very easy to implement, lightweight, and works well when all backend servers have roughly identical computing power and storage."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Does not account for server load or request processing time. If one request requires heavy computation and another is trivial, servers can become unevenly burdened over time."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// Conceptual Python implementation of Round Robin
class RoundRobinBalancer:
    def __init__(self, servers):
        self.servers = servers
        self.index = 0

    def get_server(self):
        server = self.servers[self.index]
        self.index = (self.index + 1) % len(self.servers)
        return server"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Least Connections"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Least Connections is a dynamic load balancing algorithm that directs traffic to the server with the fewest active connections currently open. This is particularly useful in environments where request processing times vary significantly, such as long-lived WebSocket connections, database queries, or file downloads."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Adapts dynamically to changing server loads. Prevents overloading servers that are handling long-running requests."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Requires the load balancer to track the state and active connection count of every server, introducing minor computational overhead."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Conceptual Python implementation of Least Connections
class LeastConnectionsBalancer:
    def __init__(self, servers):
        # Dictionary mapping server to active connection count
        self.connections = {server: 0 for server in servers}

    def get_server(self):
        # Find the server with the minimum connections
        target_server = min(self.connections, key=self.connections.get)
        self.connections[target_server] += 1
        return target_server

    def release_server(self, server):
        if self.connections[server] > 0:
            self.connections[server] -= 1"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. IP Hash"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The IP Hash algorithm takes the client's source IP address and runs it through a cryptographic hashing function to map the request to a specific server. Because the same client IP will consistently generate the same hash value, requests from that specific client are always routed to the same backend server."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Ensures session persistence (sticky sessions). Useful when applications need to maintain client state in server memory or local cache without utilizing an external session store like Redis."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Can lead to traffic imbalances if a large group of clients behind a corporate NAT or proxy share the same public IP address."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import hashlib

class IPHashBalancer:
    def __init__(self, servers):
        self.servers = servers

    def get_server(self, client_ip):
        # Hash the client IP address
        hash_object = hashlib.md5(client_ip.encode())
        hash_int = int(hash_object.hexdigest(), 16)

        # Map hash integer to server index
        server_index = hash_int % len(self.servers)
        return self.servers[server_index]"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selecting the right algorithm depends heavily on your system requirements. Use Round Robin for stateless, homogeneous servers with quick request turnaround. Choose Least Connections when request durations vary drastically. Opt for IP Hash when session state must be preserved directly on the backend server. Modern distributed systems often combine these strategies with health checks to ensure seamless failover."}]
